# ─── Start here ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing in this file is required to run a walk. Run
#
#   pnpm doctor
#
# to see which capabilities this machine already has, which are missing, and
# exactly what degrades without each one — it makes a real request against any
# key you set, so "set" and "working" are told apart before a walk depends on
# it. The same report renders at /setup on a local `pnpm dev`.

# ─── Hub ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Absolute URL the hub is served from. Only needed so the OpenGraph image
# resolves; defaults to http://localhost:3000.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ─── Target-app credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Walkthroughs run against other teams' environments, each with its own gate.
# Reference these from apps/hub/projects.json as $VAR_NAME placeholders — never
# put a literal password in that file, it is committed.
#
# One variable per app, upper-snake-case:
#   ACME_STAGING_PASSWORD=
#   ACME_MEMBER_PASSWORD=
#   FIELDNOTE_MEMBER_PASSWORD=
#   ACME_API_TOKEN=

# ─── Generated illustration (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Needed only to generate art, never to read it: every plate is committed, so a
# clone renders completely with none of this set.
#
# AND ONLY NEEDED BY AN AGENT THAT CANNOT DRAW. The walk is run by a model
# already — it writes every persona bio and finding with no key at all. The one
# thing a coding agent cannot return from a tool call is a PNG of a face, which
# is the entire reason this section exists. A host that CAN generate an image
# should write the plate itself and leave all of this empty.
#
#   WALKTHROUGH_IMAGE_PROVIDER=host   the agent draws the plates; no key used
#   WALKTHROUGH_IMAGE_PROVIDER=api    always use the provider below
#   WALKTHROUGH_IMAGE_PROVIDER=none   opt out; missing art stops being a gap
#
# Left unset, the host is detected (CLAUDECODE / CODEX_SANDBOX) and an unknown
# host is never assumed to have a capability it did not claim.
WALKTHROUGH_IMAGE_PROVIDER=
#
#   scripts/generate-creatives.mjs      the shared stipple plates in public/art
#   scripts/generate-persona-art.mjs    persona portraits and scene-setters
#   scripts/generate-persona-moments.mjs  moment shots (needs text too)
#   scripts/render-persona-story.mjs    narration for the story video
#
# DEPLOYMENT NAMES ARE NOT MODEL NAMES. On Azure the deployment in the URL
# selects the model and naming a model in the request body is rejected, so put
# whatever you actually provisioned here. There is no sensible default.
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=
AZURE_OPENAI_IMAGE_DEPLOYMENT=
AZURE_OPENAI_TEXT_DEPLOYMENT=
AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2025-04-01-preview

# ─── Story-video narration ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and render-persona-story.mjs switches to gpt-4o-mini-tts on its own.
# Leave it empty and narration is spoken by a local Kokoro model whose weights
# download on first use to ~/.cache/walkthrough-studio/ — nothing to install,
# and nothing 350 MB in the repo.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_TTS_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini-tts

# ffmpeg and uv are required either way (uv runs the local synthesis helper, so
# Python is not a dependency of this repo). Hear the candidate voices with:
#   node scripts/tts-sample.mjs
